If the central display has been exposed to direct sunlight, allow it to cool down before touching it for a long time. Overview of the MBUX multimedia system multimedia system MBUX stands for Mercedes-Benz User Experience. Touch Control and control panel for the MBUX multimedia system MBUX stands for Mercedes-Benz User Experience. The central display with touch functionality3 Switch panel with: Fingerprint sensor Switches the MBUX multimedia system on or off a Switches sound on or off Adjusts the volume Further operating options: Conducting a dialog with the MBUX voice assistant. Operating functions contact-free with the MBUX Interior Assistant. The interaction then follows intelligently, reactively or with hand or head movements. You can find further information about operation as well as about applications and services in the Digital Operators Manual. Anti-theft protection This device is equipped with technical provisions to protect it against theft. Further information on anti-theft protection can be obtained at an authorized Mercedes Benz Center. Zero layer The function of the zero layer Your software can be upgraded to a more current version at a later date. The zero layers provide you with dynamic content from the MBUX multimedia system and is used to quickly access and control the applications you use. When you select on the central display, the digital map with the applications appears in the lower display area. Compared to the home screen with a classic menu, the steps required to call up the applications are reduced. You can switch between the zero layers and the home screen with a classic menu. The applications can be hidden from the display area and shown again. The zero layers provide the following modules and applications: Navigation module In the expanded view you can, for example, display the route overview, switch on the display of traffic information and make settings for View (map), Messages & Acoustic Signals, and Route. Entertainment (media, radio) and telephone When the lower display area is shown, the entertainment sources are always displayed. A mobile phone must be connected to the MBUX multimedia system for the phone to be displayed. Active applications Active applications The lower display area shows an activ', 'images': [], 'rank': 2}, {'url': 'https://mercedes-benz-sclass-guide.flashdigest.net/mastering-the-mercedes-benz-s-class-infotainment-system-a-comprehensive-guide/', 'content': \"Skip to content July 6, 2025 9:02:12 PM About Us Contact Us Privacy Policy Terms of Service Cookie Policy/GDPR December 14, 2024 | by mercedesbenz-sclassguide Mastering the Mercedes-Benz S-Class Infotainment System: A Comprehensive Guide The Mercedes-Benz S-Class Guide provides a comprehensive overview of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class's sophisticated infotainment system, which embodies the brand's commitment to luxury and innovation. At its heart is the Mercedes-Benz User Experience (MBUX) interface, a highly adaptable system that offers intuitive control through voice, touch, or gesture, ensuring drivers can manage vehicle functions without compromising focus on the road. The MBUX system includes a high-resolution display for navigation, entertainment, and personalized settings, with split-screen capabilities in certain models for multitasking. It features advanced voice control that interprets natural language, haptic feedback, and gesture controls for hands-free operation. Integration with smartphones provides seamless connectivity, real-time updates, and access to personalized entertainment options. The system's predictive services anticipate needs like maintenance reminders or charging station suggestions. Augmented reality navigation, emergency call services, and parking assistants enhance safety while maintaining the vehicle's luxury ambiance. The Mercedes-Benz S-Class Guide also covers the infotainment system's connectivity features, including high-speed LTE for accessing apps, music services, and online platforms, making it a hub for driver assistance technologies that offer real-time traffic data and personalized settings based on facial recognition. The guide underscores the Mercedes-Benz S-Class as a pinnacle of automotive innovation, combining functionality with refined elegance to deliver an unmatched in-car experience tailored to individual preferences and needs. Explore the sophisticated realm of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class with an in-depth analysis of its cutting-edge infotainment system. This article takes you through the intricacies of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class Guide, highlighting the seamless interface and core features that elevate this luxury vehicles user experience. From advanced connectivity options to unparalleled assistance and safety features, understand what truly sets the Mercedes-Benz S-Class apart in the realm of automotive innovation. Additionally, discover how personalization and customization options tailor the driving experience to individual preferences. Join us as we delve into the comprehensive features that make the Mercedes-Benz S-Class infotainment system a marvel of modern technology. Unveiling the Mercedes-Benz S-Class Infotainment System: An Overview Navigating the Interface: The User Experience of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class Guide Core Features and Capabilities: What Sets the Mercedes-Benz S-Class Apart Advanced Technologies: Connectivity, Assistance, and Safety in the S-Class Infotainment Personalization and Customization Options for Mercedes-Benz S-Class Owners Unveiling the Mercedes-Benz S-Class Infotainment System: An Overview The Mercedes-Benz S-Class infotainment system is a testament to the brands commitment to innovation and luxury, offering drivers an intuitive and sophisticated interface to manage various vehicle functions. At the heart of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class guide lies the state-of-the-art COMAND infotainment platform, which features a high-resolution display that seamlessly integrates with the vehicles systems, allowing for navigation, entertainment, and vehicle customization. The dual screens available in some models provide a split-screen functionality, enabling drivers to access different applications simultaneously, thus enhancing multitasking capabilities without compromising on the clarity of the interface. The Mercedes-Benz S-Class guide also highlights the systems advanced voice control and natural language understanding, which allows users to interact with\", 'images': [], 'rank': 3}, {'url': 'https://mercedes-world.com/news/mbux-mercedes-benz-user-experience-infotainment-review', 'content': 'MBUX Mercedes-Benz User Experience Infotainment Review | Mercedes-Benz Worldwide Mercedes-Benz Worldwide Menu News A-Class B-Class C-Class E-Class S-Class G-Class V-Class X-Class T-Class CLA CLE CLS CLK GLA GLB GLC GLE GLS SL SLC Citan Sprinter AMG GT Maybach EQ Unimog Trucks F1 Concept Car MBUX Mercedes-Benz User Experience Infotainment Review Mercedes-Benz | Mercedes-AMG 02/09/2021 No Comments Mercedes-Benz | Mercedes-AMG Click to rate this post [Total: 1 Average: 5 ] Take a tour through the updated version of MBUX found inside the new Mercedes-Benz S-Class. We show you all the screens and various features within Mercedes latest infotainment system here. Mercedes-Benz MBUX Infotainment Review Just like the 2021 Mercedes-Benz S-Class tries to lead the way when it comes to interior luxury, it also aims to be a pioneer in infotainment technology. To accomplish this, Mercedes rolled out a new version of its MBUX software with an also-new screen to go with it. Weve had misgivings about MBUX in the past. Most of them revolve around the user interface being difficult to navigate, forcing you to take far too many swipes/steps to execute what might be a simple task in other infotainment systems. Luckily for us, Mercedes attended to this exact complaint with the new version of MBUX. Instead of a revolving door of possible menus to swipe through, the new design takes a one-screen approach by including icons for every possible submenu on the main home screen. Yeehaw! The new one-screen strategy is made possible thanks to both a new hardware design and, crucially, a new user-interface philosophy. All previous MBUX software played on a widescreen format that showed only three giant icons on its home screen at a time. It looked pretty, but was frustrating to use, as you had to constantly swipe back and forth through the carousel of icons. Thats gone now, replaced by a 12.8-inch portrait-oriented OLED that uses its extra real estate more efficiently. There are eight icons instead of three, which is a more useful and typical number, but because the screen is so large, the new UI doesnt abandon the previous screens aesthetic advantage. Your frequently used options are now staring you directly in the face upon startup in an easy-to-read format, which is just the way it should be. To our delight, Mercedes didnt fix what wasnt broken. If you were familiar with the look and feel of MBUX in other modern Mercedes products, youll also be familiar with this infotainments finer points. Youll also be hard pressed to run across a nicer looking interface than MBUX, as it communicates the proper Mercedes feeling of luxury and excess through the pretty graphics and transitions. Mercedes attempted to retain the physical feel of luxury with a haptic response to your screen inputs, too. Its relatively weak haptic feedback, but it does improve the experience over a screen without it. Just like we mentioned in our full S-Class interior review, many of Mercedes top-notch buttons and switches are deeply missed here. The touchscreen climate controls are easy to use on the MBUX screen, but they offer none of the charm or feel were accustomed to from Mercedes traditionally great switchgear. What we get instead are gadgets and gizmos aplenty. Seriously, youre probably going to be discovering new features and capabilities within this infotainment system for months, if not years, after you buy the car. Even if youve disliked voice controls in previous cars, youll want to give Mercedes voice assistant a go. Its even more capable in this latest version of MBUX, expanding its ability to understand context and have a dialogue in the car. You can ask it about buildings in your area (the car understands where it is), random general knowledge questions, and itll even provide examples of animal noises if you want it to. Basically, the system is getting closer to Apples Siri or Googles Assistant with each passing year. Riders in the backseat will have just as much control and a', 'images': [], 'rank': 4}, {'url': 'https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a33239124/2021-mercedes-s-class-mbux-3d-display/', 'content': 'Skip to Content Global Nav Search SUBSCRIBE SIGN IN RESEARCH CARS EXPERT REVIEWS SHOPPING TOOLS WHATS MY CAR WORTH? EXPERT-TESTED GEAR NEWS + STORIES MAINTENANCE HomeNews 2021 Mercedes-Benz S-Class Gets Updated Infotainment, 3D Display The next-generation MBUX infotainment system will have facial recognition, a fingerprint scanner, and more voice-activated features. By Roberto BaldwinUpdated: Jul 8, 2020 SAVE ARTICLE MERCEDES-BENZ Mercedes has unveiled features of the next-generation MBUX infotainment system coming to the new S-class, including a 3D instrument cluster, \"Hey Mercedes\" access for all four passengers, and augmented reality in the head-up display. The 2021 S-class is expected to be unveiled later this year. UPDATE, 7/8/20: During a question and answer sesssion, Gorden Wagener, chief design officer of Daimler, said that there is a high probability that we\\'ll see the portrait display that\\'s in the upcoming S-class eventually make its way into all new Mercedes in the future. Wagener also noted that the touchpad that has been part of the Mercedes center console for years is going away. The touchscreen is now in a position that Wagener says doesn\\'t require any stretching or discomfort to use, and therefore there is no use for the touchpad. We also learned that the new HUD and profile log-in features were born out of customer feedback. Drivers were telling the automaker that they wanted a better and more secure way to make sure that when they were driving, the car was tuned to their needs. Mercedes went all in when it introduced voice recognition to its MBUX infotainment system. It took what has worked in the home and in our pockets from systems by Amazon, Apple, and Google and transferred it to the road. Now it\\'s pushing its first big update via the upcoming S-class. In addition to the expected increase in voice controls, it\\'s also taking over more of the features that were formerly controlled by hardware buttons. The new S-class is slated to make its debut later this year. MERCEDES-BENZ The 3D instrument cluster, Mercedes says, uses stereo cameras to track the driver\\'s eyes and will be able to create a 3D-looking interface. The system uses the traditional LCD display combined with an LCD aperture grille that sits a few millimeters ahead of the display. As the driver\\'s head moves, the aperture grille adjusts itself so that the right and left eye each see something slightly different, creating a 3D stereoscopic effect. For drivers not keen on a three-dimensional dash cluster, Mercedes says the feature can be shut off. PREPARE YOURSELF FOR THE NEXT S-CLASS 2021 Mercedes S-class Shows Its Face, Officially 2021 Mercedes S-Class Leaked, Has Modern Interior We Drive Every Generation of the Mercedes S-Class For the new vehicle, the automaker has moved the infotainment screen down from the dash and into the center stack. Mercedes says that the 12.8-inch OLED display, along with new swiping and other gesture controls, has replaced an additional 27 hard keys in the vehicle. So if you\\'re a fan of physical knobs and buttons, we have bad news for you. The automaker has made sure, at least, that the climate controls are always available on the massive screen. ADVERTISEMENT - CONTINUE READING BELOW The screen\\'s layout does seem like an improvement over the current version of MBUX with more of a tablet grid layout instead of having to swipe left and right to access major features. Mercedes also stated that the new system will have 50 percent more processing power than its predecessor, which might reduce some of the latency issues we\\'ve experienced while using the current system. The voice assistant has more information for drivers, including pointing users to the location of the first aid kit and walking passengers through Bluetooth pairing. Mercedes also added more natural conversational ability to the system so it can answer \"general knowledge\" questions or understand commands and requests in 27 languages. MERCEDES-BENZ', 'images': [], 'rank': 5}, {'url': 'https://www.arrowheadmb.com/blog/infotainment-features-in-the-2022-mercedes-benz-s-class/', 'content': '[not extracted] Content limit reached', 'rank': 6}, {'url': 'https://robbreport.com/motors/cars/mercedes-benz-s-class-mbux-infotainment-system-2933873/', 'content': '[not extracted] Content limit reached', 'rank': 7}, {'url': 'https://www.mercedesbenzofwashington.com/the-2021-mercedes-s-class-is-giving-you-the-3-d-experience-with-their-new-infotainment-system/', 'content': '[not extracted] Content limit reached', 'rank': 8}]",
